<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panama Canal Zone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> Zone was a strip of territory about ten miles wide along the Panama Canal, administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1903 to 1979 under the Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty. It had its own government, courts, police and schools, and its American residents, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zonians">Zonians</a>, lived largely apart from Panamanians. The riots of January 1964 over the right to fly the Panamanian flag hastened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a>, under which the zone was abolished on 1 October 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone.jpg" alt="Panama Canal Zone" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BMacZero · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Panama_Canal_Zone.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Panama Canal Zone, also known simply as the Canal Zone, was a concession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-panama">Isthmus of Panama</a> that existed from 1903 to 1979. It consisted of the Panama Canal and an area generally extending five miles on each side of the centreline, but excluding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a> and Colón. Its capital was Balboa. The territory covered 553 square miles and spanned two provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, Colón and Panamá. The lakes created to assure a steady supply of water for the locks were included. The zone had no formal boundary restrictions on Panamanians, and in Panama City one could enter it simply by crossing a street.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Proposals for a canal across the isthmus go back to 1529, when Álvaro de Saavedra Cerón suggested four routes, but Spanish officials judged the work beyond 16th-century technology. The zone was created on 18 November 1903 from Panamanian territory by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hay-bunau-varilla-treaty">Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty</a>. Panama received US$10 million and annual payments of US$250,000, and the zone was formally turned over on 4 May 1904. American construction concluded in August 1914 with the opening of the canal to commercial traffic. An executive order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> placed governance under the Secretary of War, giving the canal an essentially military arrangement.</p><p>During construction and into the 1940s the labour force was divided into a gold roll and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> roll. In practice race decided the placing: American and northern European white workers went on the gold roll, and Black and southern European workers, the Black workers mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, on the silver roll. Until 1945 the zone operated under a Jim Crow system, with separate entrances at the post office. Black workers were banned from unionising after the strike of 1920, and segregation in housing and schooling persisted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The zone, a political exclave with its own courts, police and civil government that cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> geographically in half, became a cause of conflict. Major rioting occurred on 21 May 1958 and 3 November 1959, and serious rioting in January 1964 led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to ease its controls, allowing Panamanian flags to fly beside the American flag. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a>, agreed in 1977, abolished the zone on 1 October 1979, when most of its land was transferred to Panama. The Panama Canal Commission was created to ease the transition, and the canal was fully turned over to Panama in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Panama Canal Zone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1903 treaty granting the United States the <b>Canal Zone</b> was signed for Panama by a Frenchman, Philippe Bunau-Varilla, before the Panamanian delegation had reached Washington.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
